The **Timms Review** is the first full review of **Personal Independence Payment (PIP)** since the benefit began in 2013. It is named after **Sir Stephen Timms**, the Minister for Social Security and Disability, who was one of its 3 co-chairs.

The review published its **final report** on **24 November 2026**. This article explains what it found, what it recommends and what happens next.

{% callout type="info" title="The short version" %}
- **Nothing has changed yet.** These are recommendations, not government policy
- The review says PIP is **highly valued**, but the system is **no longer fit for purpose**
- PIP should stay a **cash benefit** that is **not means-tested** and is open to people **in or out of work**
- It makes **15 recommendations** to improve PIP now, such as **fewer reviews** for lifelong conditions and a **simpler form**
- It also sets out a **reformed benefit** for the future, with a new **impact assessment** that looks at your **extra costs**
- The government will now decide what to do
{% /callout %}

## Why the review happened

PIP replaced Disability Living Allowance (DLA) for adults in 2013. In 13 years, **PIP itself had never been fully reviewed**.

The Timms Review came out of a debate in Parliament in July 2025, when the Universal Credit Act was being passed. The government had planned changes to who could get PIP, but there was strong opposition from disabled people, their organisations and MPs. So the government agreed to a review that disabled people would help to shape.

## Who ran it and how

The review was led by **3 co-chairs**: **Sir Stephen Timms**, **Sharon Brennan** and **Dr Clenton Farquharson CBE**. They worked with **12 steering group members**, chosen from more than 340 applicants. Almost all of them have lived experience of disability or long-term ill health.

The review was **co-produced**, which means disabled people helped decide how it worked and what it recommended. Each member had an equal vote, and decisions needed a **two-thirds majority**.

It heard from **more than 40,000 people** through:

- a **call for evidence**, with **38,713 responses**
- **Workshop in a Box**: 138 workshops run by local groups, with about **1,240 people**
- a **survey** of **2,039** working-age disabled people in England and Wales, run by NatCen
- **26 sessions** with experts, including people with lived experience and officials from other countries
- **15 workshops** with **224 people** to test the recommendations, and **roundtables** with organisations

## What people told the review

### PIP is valued

- around **half** of call for evidence responses were positive about PIP as a benefit
- in the survey, **82%** of people getting PIP said it had helped them in the last year
- people use it most for **travel**, **equipment and aids**, and **personal assistance**

### The process is not

- **more than 90%** of responses about the claiming process were negative
- about **70%** raised problems with the **assessment**, and about **45%** said it does not work for **conditions that change from day to day**
- about **60%** described the process harming their **mental or emotional wellbeing**
- people often called the assessment **"dehumanising"**, and said they felt judged or "caught out"
- many said they were afraid to **exercise, volunteer or work** in case it was seen as proof they no longer needed PIP

### Other findings

- among disabled people **not getting PIP or DLA**, **64%** had **never applied**, mainly because they believed they would not qualify, often for reasons that are not part of the rules, such as working or having savings
- **41%** wanted PIP to stay **cash only**, **55%** wanted a mix of **cash and services**, and only **4%** wanted **services only**
- the most wanted extra help was **healthcare referrals**, **travel support**, **advice** and **equipment**
- **4 in 5** people awarded PIP have conditions in **more than one** health group

## The main conclusion

The report agrees with the interim report: **PIP remains a valued and important benefit, but the system through which it is delivered is no longer fit for purpose.**

The steering group agreed that the purpose of PIP is to help D/deaf and disabled people and people with long-term health conditions, and to reduce the inequalities they face in everyday life, through a **contribution towards the extra costs** of disability.

Its clear position is that PIP should:

- stay a **cash award**
- stay **not means-tested** and **not taxed**
- stay open to people **in and out of work**
- **not be conditional**: getting PIP should not depend on taking up support, or on how much you work or take part in everyday life

The review wants to move away from a system that only looks at **what people cannot do**, to one that understands **what people can and want to do**, the **barriers** they face and their **extra costs**.

## 15 recommendations to improve PIP now

These are changes the review thinks can be made within the current system, while a reformed benefit is developed.

### Making it easier to apply and get PIP

1. **Fewer award reviews** for people with **lifelong or degenerative conditions**. They should get ongoing awards with a **light-touch check-in**, and reviews should focus on **what has changed**, using forms filled in with your previous answers
2. A **simpler PIP2 form** ("How your disability affects you") in plain language, which better captures **fluctuating conditions** and asks what you **can** do, not only what you cannot
3. **Clearer, more respectful letters**, which explain how a decision was reached and the evidence used
4. Better **reasonable adjustments**, recorded **once** so you do not have to keep asking
5. **Independent advocacy**, funded by DWP but independent of it, from the start of a claim through to appeals
6. **Dedicated case management teams** for people with the highest support needs, with a consistent point of contact
7. Review the **28-day hospital rule**, so some support can continue during long hospital stays, and **scrap the "linking rule"** that adds together stays 28 days or less apart

### Fairer assessments and decisions

8. A clear framework for how decisions are made and challenged. This includes giving you a copy of your **assessment report and recording** automatically, and explaining when a decision goes against your medical evidence
9. **Better training** for assessors, including on how disability affects daily life and trauma-informed practice, and making the assessor role a **recognised profession**
10. Review **mandatory reconsiderations**, and use what is learned from reconsiderations and appeals to get more decisions **right first time**
11. **Better use of evidence**, including **paper-based decisions** where there is enough evidence, letting you ask DWP to get evidence for you, and making clear you **should not pay your GP** for evidence
12. Better assessment of **fluctuating conditions**. If better guidance and training do not work, DWP should review the current rules, including the rule about **more than 50% of days**
13. A **choice of assessment method** (face-to-face, phone, video or paper), with more **face-to-face** capacity

### Building for the future

14. More **accessible digital services**, including a secure **online portal** where you can apply, upload evidence and track your claim. **Paper and phone** options must stay
15. **Better data** on who applies for PIP, to find and tackle unfair differences between groups, with strong data protection

## A reformed benefit for the future

The review also sets out a longer-term plan for a **reformed benefit**. It would keep what people value about PIP while changing how it works. Under the plan:

- **A light-touch first step.** Everyone would start with a **short, simple form**, online, by phone or on paper, to check basic eligibility and choose the right route
- **A single digital profile.** With your permission, your evidence would be kept in one place, so you **tell DWP once**. People who cannot use the internet would get the same service through a case manager
- **Two fast-track routes** with **no full assessment**: one for people receiving **end-of-life care**, and one for people with **serious, well-evidenced conditions**, including lifelong conditions where needs will not reduce
- **A new impact assessment** for everyone else. It would look at how your condition affects your daily life over time, your **barriers**, your **extra costs** and your **aspirations**
- **An extra costs element.** It would ask about your **needs, not your spending**. You would **not** have to work out your costs or **provide receipts**
- **A cash-first award.** Cash would stay the main part. Payments could include **regular payments**, **bands** more closely linked to extra costs, and **one-off payments** for larger costs. There would be **no vouchers**
- **Optional services.** You could choose to swap part of your cash for a **service**, similar to the Motability Scheme, and switch back without delay. **Declining** services would **not** affect your award
- **An optional supportive conversation**, run locally, to help you find other support, such as Centres for Independent Living
- **Support throughout**, including reasonable adjustments, peer support and advocacy

{% callout type="warn" title="Who gets the reformed benefit could change" %}
The review's terms of reference say PIP spending must stay within the **Office for Budget Responsibility's forecast**. The review says new eligibility rules are likely to need a **"rebalancing"**, so that people with the highest extra costs get the most support, and the benefit is **not for people with no extra costs** from disability. This could mean some people get more and some get less. The review says any new rules must be independently checked, tested with disabled people and brought in with **transitional protections**.
{% /callout %}

The review also says that taking part in **family life, education, volunteering or work** should **not**, in itself, be treated as evidence that your needs have reduced.

## The Work Capability Assessment

The government already plans to **abolish the Work Capability Assessment (WCA)** for Universal Credit. PIP would then become the **single gateway** to the Universal Credit health element. Read about [changes to health and disability benefits](/benefits/universal-credit-health/changes/).

The review says the WCA should **only** be abolished if there are **strong safeguards**, so people who cannot work are not given work-related requirements. Not all of the steering group agreed with the single gateway, and the review warns that PIP may be **asked to do too much**.

## Motability

The review says the **Motability Scheme** is an important part of how PIP helps people get around, and it must **stay voluntary**. Losing the enhanced mobility rate can mean losing your car, so decisions must be right first time. Some members raised concerns about recent changes to the scheme, and the review suggests further work on how it is run.

## Where the steering group disagreed

Most recommendations were agreed by the majority. The report records some disagreements:

- **Jean-André Prager** disagreed that PIP should never be conditional. He thinks that for some groups, PIP should depend on engagement and taking part in society, where reasonable. He also said there are limits to what co-production can decide
- **Dr Mark Fosbrook** raised concerns about **one-off payments**, and said extra safeguards may be needed for people in poverty or who are vulnerable

## What happens next

- The report goes to the **Secretary of State for Work and Pensions**, who will decide what to do
- The review asks the government to publish a **formal response**, saying which recommendations it accepts and when
- Some improvements could be made **without new laws**. Others, including the reformed benefit, would need **new laws** and testing
- The review wants changes brought in **step by step**, with **protections** for people moving across. It points to **transition payments** used in Northern Ireland as an example
- It recommends an **independent evaluation** of the review itself

At the time of writing, the government has not yet responded. Read our article on the [Budget 2026](/articles/budget-2026-disabled-people-and-benefits/), where the government said it would respond to the review later.

### Scotland, Wales and Northern Ireland

PIP is run by DWP in **England and Wales**, and by the Department for Communities in **Northern Ireland**. In **Scotland**, PIP has been replaced by [Adult Disability Payment](/benefits/adp/). The review asks the government to think about how its recommendations would work across the UK.

## Timeline

- **July 2025:** the review is promised during a debate on the Universal Credit Act
- **October 2025:** terms of reference published, and co-chairs appointed
- **January 2026:** 12 steering group members appointed
- **19 March to 28 May 2026:** call for evidence (38,713 responses)
- **8 June to 17 July 2026:** Workshop in a Box
- **July 2026:** interim report says PIP is no longer fit for purpose
- **September 2026:** emerging recommendations published
- **15 September to 7 October 2026:** workshops to test the recommendations
- **24 November 2026:** final report published

## What this means for you now

- **Nothing changes yet.** Claim PIP in the usual way if you think you qualify. The survey found many people who have never applied think they would not qualify, often for reasons that are not part of the rules
- If you already get PIP, **keep reporting changes** as normal. Read about [reporting a change of circumstances](/benefits/pip/change-of-circumstances/)
- If you disagree with a decision, you can still [challenge it](/benefits/pip/challenge-a-decision/)
- Check how the activities apply to you with our free [PIP points self-check](/benefits/pip/points-checker/). Nothing leaves your device
- Read the **Easy Read** version of the report on GOV.UK, listed in the sources below

We will update this article when the government responds.

